s surrogate architectures, for aerodynamic drag prediction across the three body styles (fastback, notchback, estateback).\n\n- **H1 (primary \u2014 data scaling):** For each architecture, predictive accuracy (R\u00b2\u2191, RMSE\u2193) improves monotonically with training-set size, following a learning curve with diminishing returns / saturation near the full dataset.\n- **H2 (primary \u2014 architecture dependence):** The *shape* of the learning curve differs across architectures: small-sample and large-sample regimes favor different models (e.g., GP/linear strong at small n; NN/GBM catch up and dominate at large n), and asymptotic accuracy at N_full differs.\n- **H3 (secondary \u2014 body-style dependence):** Performance and/or curve shape differ across body styles (some styles harder or higher-variance), detectable in the per-style breakdown.\n\n**Success criteria:** H1 supported if median R\u00b2 rises (RMSE falls) across the size grid with a visible saturation knee. H2 supported if the ordering of architectures changes with n (curve crossing) or asymptotic R\u00b2 differs beyond the CV confidence interval. H3 supported if per-style curves are non-parallel / non-overlapping.\n\n## 2) Dataset and data-acquisition plan\n\nAnchors taken directly from the task statement: **26 continuous input parameters**, **3 body styles** (fastback, notchback, estateback), **target = aerodynamic drag** (drag coefficient). Everything else is *to-verify* before execution.\n\n**Data acquisition (must happen before any run):** obtain DrivAerNet++ from the authors' official repository / Kaggle release once a network is available. Because the current sandbox has no network and no dataset, the experiment cannot be executed here; the design below is fully specified so it is ready to run the moment data + dependencies are available.\n\n**Verification checklist (block execution until all confirmed):**\n- [ ] **N** \u2014 total number of configurations; per-body-style counts (for stratification proportions).\n- [ ] **Parameter list & units** \u2014 the exact 26 features, their physical meaning, units, and value ranges (for standardization checks and to rule out constant/degenerate columns).\n- [ ] **Target definition** \u2014 drag coefficient CDx vs. drag force; single scalar vs. multiple drag-related outputs (we use one scalar drag target); whether values are dimensionless.\n- [ ] **Canonical splits** \u2014 whether the release ships a train/test split; if yes, decide (default: create our *own* splits to control the size sweep; use the canonical split only as a secondary sanity check).\n- [ ] **License / access terms** \u2014 confirm the license permits the intended use and redistribution of derived artifacts.\n- [ ] **Data quality** \u2014 missing values, duplicates, outliers; confirm the 26 inputs are finite.\n\n## 3) Surrogate-model set and hyperparameter search spaces\n\nChosen to span inductive biases for a 26-D continuous regression, all CPU-feasible:\n\n- **Ridge / linear (baseline):** lower bound. HP: `alpha \u2208 {1e-3, 1e-2, 1e-1, 1, 10, 100}` (log grid).\n- **k-NN (baseline):** local, non-parametric reference. HP: `k \u2208 {3, 5, 10, 20, 50}`, `weights \u2208 {uniform, distance}`.\n- **Random Forest (RF):** robust nonlinear tabular model. HP: `n_estimators \u2208 {200, 500}`, `max_depth \u2208 {10, 20, None}`, `min_samples_leaf \u2208 {1, 2, 5}`.\n- **Gradient Boosting (XGBoost or LightGBM):** strong tabular learner, expected to benefit from large n. HP: `n_estimators \u2208 {200, 500, 1000}`, `max_depth \u2208 {3, 6, 9}`, `learning_rate \u2208 {0.03, 0.1}`, `subsample \u2208 {0.8, 1.0}`.\n- **Gaussian Process (RBF + WhiteKernel):** principled small-data model with uncertainty; **restricted to small n** (see \u00a78). HP: `length_scale \u2208 {0.3, 1.0, 3.0}` (ARD off to save cost), `noise_level \u2208 {1e-3, 1e-2, 1e-1}`.\n- **Multilayer Perceptron (small FC NN):** the \"neural surrogate\" matching the DrivAerNet lineage; the model expected to scale best. HP: `hidden_layer_sizes \u2208 {(128,64), (256,128), (256,128,64)}`, `activation = relu`, `alpha (L2) \u2208 {1e-4, 1e-3, 1e-2}`, `max_iter` with early stopping, `learning_rate_init \u2208 {1e-3, 1e-2}`.\n\nAll HPs are tuned **per architecture, per training size, inside the same CV folds** (\u00a76, \u00a77). Search is bounded (small grids) and uses random search with a fixed seed if a full grid is too costly.\n\n## 4) Training-set-size sweep\n\n- **Grid (log-spaced), with placeholders:** `{n_min, \u2026, N_full}` where `n_min` and `N_full` are fixed at data-verification time. Illustrative concrete grid pending N: `{50, 100, 200, 500, 1000, 2000, 5000, 10000, 20000, N_full}` \u2014 final set = the log-spaced points \u2264 N_full plus N_full itself. GP is only evaluated on the small-n points (\u2264 ~2000, see \u00a78).\n- **Construction \u2014 nested, seeded, stratified:**\n  1. First split the full dataset into one **training pool** and one **held-out test set** (default 80/20, stratified by body style, fixed seed) so the test set is *identical across all sizes and all architectures*.\n  2. Each size-n training subset is a **nested** subsample of the training pool (size n \u2282 size 2n \u2282 \u2026), guaranteeing comparable learning curves.\n  3. Subsampling is **stratified by body style** (preserve the 3-style proportions); for the two smallest sizes, optionally use a **space-filling (Latin-hypercube) sample** of the pool to maximize coverage of the 26-D space.\n  4. All random draws use a **fixed global seed**.\n- **Distinct subsets per size:** **R = 5** independent (re-seeded) subsets per size to estimate sampling variance. (Minimum-viable fallback: R = 3.)\n\n## 5) Evaluation protocol\n\n- **Metrics (on the held-out test set):** **RMSE, R\u00b2, MAE, and relative error** (MAE / |mean(target)|, or mean |err|/|true|). R\u00b2 and RMSE are primary; MAE/relative error are robustness checks.\n- **Outer CV scheme:** For each (architecture, size,