red size, compare architectures' R\u00b2 using **paired (over the R subsets) Wilcoxon signed-rank / paired t-tests** with **Benjamini\u2013Hochberg FDR correction** across the architecture pairs \u00d7 sizes.\n- **Curve-crossing analysis:** identify sizes where the ranking of two architectures flips (visual + the sign change in paired differences) to characterize small-n vs large-n regime shifts.\n- **Formal test (chosen):** a **linear mixed-effects model** `R\u00b2 ~ log(n) + architecture + log(n):architecture + (1 | subset)` \u2014 this jointly captures the size effect (H1), main architecture effect, and the **size\u00d7architecture interaction** (the core of H2: does the slope/asymptote differ by architecture?) with subset as a random effect to account for repeated subsampling. Justification: it is a single model that tests both H1 and H2, handles the repeated-measures structure, and is far cheaper/more interpretable than many post-hoc pairwise tests. (Report the interaction F-test; follow up with the pairwise FDR tests for localization.)\n- **H3:** the same mixed-effects model with a `body_style` term and `log(n):body_style` interaction (per-style setting), plus visual per-style curve comparison.\n\n## 7) Controls and fairness\n\n- **Identical preprocessing for all architectures:** same feature standardization (fit on training subset only), same missing-value policy, same target. Standardizer fit/transform strictly on training data to avoid leakage.\n- **Identical splits across architectures:** every model sees the *same* training subsets and the *same* held-out test set (same seed), so any performance difference is attributable to the model, not the data.\n- **HP re-tuning within the same CV folds:** tuning uses only the inner validation split; the outer test set is never used for selection.\n- **No data leakage:** standardizers, outlier treatment, and any scaling fit on train only; nested subsampling keeps small-n models a strict subset of large-n training data.\n- **Fixed seeds** for subsampling, HP search, and NN initialization (multiple NN seeds folded into the R repeats).\n- **Same metric definitions and same test set** for pooled and per-style comparisons.\n\n## 8) Feasibility (CPU-only, 224 cores, no GPU, no pre-installed libs)\n\n- **Prerequisite install step (not done here):** `pip install numpy pandas scikit-learn xgboost lightgbm` (and a data loader). This requires network, which is currently disabled \u2014 a hard blocker for execution in this sandbox.\n- **Parallelism:** embarrassingly parallel over (architecture \u00d7 size \u00d7 subset \u00d7 HP) \u2192 use joblib/224 cores. The sweep is I/O- and fit-bound, not GPU-bound.\n- **Model restrictions by n:** **GP is O(n\u00b3) in training** \u2192 restrict to small-n points (default n \u2264 ~2000; drop GP below that if N_full is large). **NN and GBM** are the only models that comfortably reach N_full. RF scales well throughout.\n- **Rough wall-clock estimate (order-of-magnitude, to be confirmed on real N):** Let G = number of size grid points (~10), R = 5 subsets, M = 6 architectures, K \u2248 20\u201340 inner HP trials. Total fits \u2248 G\u00b7R\u00b7M\u00b7K \u2248 10\u00b75\u00b76\u00b730 \u2248 **9,000 fits** (pooled) plus ~3\u00d7 for per-style. On 224 cores with a few-second-to-minute cost per fit (GP at small n, NN/GBM at large n the slowest), the pooled+per-style sweep is on the order of **tens of minutes to a few hours** wall-clock (heavily dependent on N_full and NN `max_iter`). This is comfortably feasible on the stated hardware.\n- **Minimum-viable sweep (if compute is tight):** reduce to grid `{n_min, ~10 mid points, N_full}` (\u22486 points), R = 3 subsets, drop k-NN and GP-above-1000, per-style on the 3 largest sizes only, and single NN seed. This preserves the ability to test H1 (trend + saturation), H2 (crossing + interaction), and H3 (per-style) at reduced variance.\n\n## 9) Limitations and out-of-scope extensions\n\n- **Design-only:** no data executed; all dataset numbers (N, features, target, splits) are placeholders pending the \u00a72 verification checklist. Conclusions are conditional on the verified dataset matching the published description.\n- **Single-target:** we evaluate one scalar drag target; multi-output / coefficient-of-drag-vector prediction is out of scope.\n- **CPU-only, no GPU:** no large deep networks or physics simulators; NNs are deliberately small.\n- **Out-of-scope extensions (not run):** **physics-informed surrogates** (e.g., embedding CFD/conservation constraints or a physics loss) \u2014 the question targets data-driven surrogates, so this is deferred; **active learning / Bayesian-optimal subset selection** (vs. our random/stratified subsampling); **surrogate-of-surrogate / ensemble of the surrogates**; **uncertainty-quantification comparison** (GP vs. MC-dropout NN) beyond reporting GP variance; and **generalization to unseen body styles** (out-of-distribution). Any of these could be a follow-up once the base scaling study is validated.\n\n---\n*This is a self-contained design proposal. It specifies a reproducible protocol but reports no executed experiments or invented outcomes.*"}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-69ffb8538d4a47f99feb1d7616082c08", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "critic_result": null, "summary": "Deliver full experiment design document for DrivAerNet++ surrogate scaling study", "kind": "ActionEvent"}
